{
  "id": "q3-grassland-gaps",
  "question": "For grassland restoration in Canada, which intervention-outcome combinations have little or no evidence, and where is the graph densest?",
  "text": "Of the 384 intervention and outcome pairings in the grassland evidence map below, 123 have at least one study, 72 of them only one, and 261 have no mapped evidence. Five interventions have no mapped evidence for any outcome: timber harvest, livestock grazing, hydrological restoration, predator and competitor control, and passive recovery. Invasive plant removal, irrigation, erosion structures and mine reclamation each have mapped evidence for only two outcomes. There is no mapped evidence for contaminants or animal behaviour; ecosystem processes are represented for only two interventions, and hydrology for one. The most studied pairings are seeding and herbicide for plant cover, with 9 studies each, and seeding for species richness, with 8. Eight more have 5 studies each: herbicide and mowing for richness and invasive species, mulch for plant cover and growth, organic amendments for soil chemistry, and seeding for abundance. Study counts show research effort, not whether a practice works.",
